### What is Predicate.not()?

Three ways to negate a predicate; the third (Java 11) is cleanest.

```java
import java.util.function.Predicate;

// Way 1: negated lambda (verbose, easy to miss the !)
trades.stream().filter(s -> !s.isBlank());

// Way 2: .negate() (readable but longer)
Predicate<String> notBlank = ((Predicate<String>) String::isBlank).negate();

// Way 3: Predicate.not() (Java 11, cleanest)
trades.stream().filter(Predicate.not(String::isBlank));
// Reads as: "filter: NOT blank"
```

### How do you use Predicate.not with other types?

```java
import java.util.function.Predicate;
import java.util.List;

// Filter non-settled trades
trades.stream().filter(Predicate.not(Trade::isSettled));

// Filter non-empty lists
accounts.stream().filter(Predicate.not(List::isEmpty));

// Compose predicates
Predicate<Trade> notSettledOrLarge = Predicate.not(Trade::isSettled)
    .or(t -> t.getAmount().compareTo(limit) > 0);
```

### What is Pattern.asPredicate vs asMatchPredicate?

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.function.Predicate;

Pattern ifscPattern = Pattern.compile("[A-Z]{4}0[A-Z0-9]{6}");

// asPredicate: find() - string CONTAINS a match
Predicate<String> containsIfsc = ifscPattern.asPredicate();
containsIfsc.test("Code: SBIN0001234 here");  // true: found within string
containsIfsc.test("no match");                 // false

// asMatchPredicate: matches() - ENTIRE string must match
Predicate<String> isIfsc = ifscPattern.asMatchPredicate();
isIfsc.test("SBIN0001234");               // true: entire string matches
isIfsc.test("Code: SBIN0001234 here");    // false: extra text!
```

### What is the comparison between asPredicate and asMatchPredicate?

| Method | Match type | Added in | Matcher method |
|--------|-----------|---------|----------------|
| asPredicate() | Partial (find) | Java 8 | matcher.find() |
| asMatchPredicate() | Full (match) | Java 11 | matcher.matches() |

### How do you use regex predicates in stream filters?

```java
List<String> validCodes = inputs.stream()
    .filter(ifscPattern.asMatchPredicate())
    .toList();

// Multiple regex filters composed
Predicate<String> isIfsc = ifscPattern.asMatchPredicate();
Predicate<String> notBlank = Predicate.not(String::isBlank);

List<String> cleanValid = inputs.stream()
    .filter(notBlank.and(isIfsc))
    .toList();
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: recompiling pattern inside filter (expensive)
accounts.stream()
    .filter(id -> Pattern.matches("SAV\\d+", id)); // recompiles every call!

// CORRECT: compile once, reuse predicate
Predicate<String> isSavings = Pattern.compile("SAV\\d+").asMatchPredicate();
accounts.stream().filter(isSavings);
```

### How does Predicate.not differ from negate()?

```java
// negate(): instance method, needs a Predicate variable
Predicate<String> blank = String::isBlank;
Predicate<String> notBlank = blank.negate();

// not(): static method, accepts method reference directly
Predicate.not(String::isBlank); // no intermediate variable needed

// Same result, different syntax
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Predicate.not() works with any method reference or lambda, not just String methods.
- Pattern compilation is expensive; compile once and reuse. The resulting Predicate is thread-safe.
- asPredicate uses find() (partial match); asMatchPredicate uses matches() (full match). Know the difference.
- Predicate.not was added in Java 11 specifically to make method reference negation cleaner.
- All Predicate variants compose with and(), or(), and negate() for complex filter logic.
